\chapter{The Pipeline Feature}\label{ch6-the-pipeline-feature}

\section{Motivation}\label{ch6-motivation}

Functional composition is the most natural way to describe a
transformation that happens in stages. When the stages are written as
nested function calls, the reader must parse them from the inside out:
\texttt{f2(\allowbreak{}f1(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
reads ``apply \texttt{f1}, then \texttt{f2},'' but the textual order is
reversed relative to the temporal order. The Pipeline feature inverts
this so that the reading direction matches the data-flow direction.

MuDSL provides the Pipeline feature so that a DSL author can write
\texttt{value | pipe(\allowbreak{}f1)\allowbreak{} | pipe(\allowbreak{}f2)\allowbreak{}}
and obtain the value
\texttt{f2(\allowbreak{}f1(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
Each \texttt{|} is a single function application. There is no scheduler,
no coroutine machinery, no runtime graph of nodes. The mechanism is a
single overloaded \texttt{operator|} that forwards its left operand into
the callable stored on its right.

This chapter describes every component of the feature: the
\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
wrapper, the \texttt{pipe(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} factory, the free
\texttt{operator|}, the \texttt{Pipeline} feature tag, and its
\texttt{Mixin:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap} static identity lift. It
then demonstrates type-changing chains, value semantics,
\texttt{constexpr} evaluation, composition with other features, and the
pitfalls that arise from operator precedence, reference captures, and
temporary lifetimes.

\section{Overview of the Mechanism}\label{ch6-overview-of-the-mechanism}

The header describes the feature in one line: ``Adds \texttt{operator|}
so values can flow through a chain of transformations.'' The canonical
usage is shown in the header comment as
\texttt{auto result = va\allowbreak{}lue | dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}f1)\allowbreak{} | dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}f2)\allowbreak{};}
with the gloss that this is equivalent to
\texttt{f2(\allowbreak{}f1(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
The rest of the chapter unpacks that equivalence.

Three components cooperate.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}f)\allowbreak{}}
constructs a
\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
object that holds a copy of \texttt{f}. The free
\texttt{operator|(\allowbreak{}T\&\&,\allowbreak{} PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
applies the held callable to its left operand. The \texttt{Pipeline}
feature tag supplies \texttt{Mixin:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap}, a
static identity function that marks the start of a chain and returns its
argument unchanged. Everything else is convention.

Because the \texttt{operator|} is left-associative,
\texttt{value | pipe(\allowbreak{}f1)\allowbreak{} | pipe(\allowbreak{}f2)\allowbreak{}}
parses as
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}value | pipe(\allowbreak{}f1)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} | pipe(\allowbreak{}f2)\allowbreak{}}.
The inner expression yields
\texttt{f1(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{}}, and the outer expression
yields
\texttt{f2(\allowbreak{}f1(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
The associativity of the operator is what makes the textual order match
the evaluation order.

The feature is opt-in. A DSL gains pipeline support only by listing
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Pipeline} among the features of
its
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
base. Without that, \texttt{wrap} is absent and the only way to use
\texttt{operator|} is to construct \texttt{PipeStage} objects directly
via \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe}. The feature tag exists
so that a DSL has a single, uniform entry point named \texttt{wrap}.

\section{The PipeStage Wrapper}\label{ch6-the-pipestage-wrapper}

A pipeline stage is any callable that accepts one argument and returns a
value. To distinguish such a callable from an arbitrary function object
in the type system, the library wraps it in
\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The wrapper is a trivial struct with a single data member.

The definition, quoted verbatim from the header, is:

\begin{code}
template <typename F> struct PipeStage
{
  F fn;
  explicit constexpr PipeStage (F f) : fn (std::move (f)) {}
};
\end{code}

The template parameter \texttt{F} is the callable's type. The member
\texttt{fn} holds the callable by value. The constructor is
\texttt{explicit}, which prevents implicit conversions from a bare
callable to a \texttt{PipeStage}; a caller must either use the
\texttt{pipe(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} factory or construct the
wrapper explicitly. The constructor is also \texttt{constexpr}, so a
stage can be constructed at compile time.

Because \texttt{fn} is held by value, the callable is copied or moved
into the stage at construction. A lambda with captured state is
therefore copied into the stage, and subsequent uses of the stage
operate on that copy. This is the intended value semantics: a stage is
self-contained and may be reused, moved, or stored without reference to
the original callable object.

The \texttt{explicit} constructor has a practical consequence. Writing
\texttt{5 | [](\allowbreak{}int x)\allowbreak{}\{ return x*2; \}} does
not compile, because the lambda cannot implicitly become a
\texttt{PipeStage}. The \texttt{pipe(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
factory exists precisely to make the construction concise without
removing the \texttt{explicit} guarantee from the wrapper itself. A user
who wants a named, reusable stage can write
\texttt{auto dbl = dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}[](\allowbreak{}int x)\allowbreak{}\{ return x*2; \})\allowbreak{};}
and then apply it many times.

\section{The pipe() Factory}\label{ch6-the-pipe-factory}

The factory is a one-line function template that deduces \texttt{F} and
constructs a
\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}decay\_\allowbreak{}t\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Quoted verbatim:

\begin{code}
template <typename F>
constexpr auto
pipe (F &&f)
{
  return PipeStage<std::decay_t<F>>{ std::forward<F> (f) };
}
\end{code}

The use of
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}decay\_\allowbreak{}t\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
as the template argument to \texttt{PipeStage} strips references and
cv-qualifiers from the deduced type, so \texttt{pipe} always produces a
stage that owns its callable by value. A lambda passed to \texttt{pipe}
is therefore copied (or moved, if the argument is an rvalue) into the
stage, never held by reference. The
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}forward\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f)\allowbreak{}}
inside the braced initializer then forwards the argument to the
\texttt{PipeStage} constructor, preserving the value category for the
inner move or copy.

The factory is \texttt{constexpr}, so a stage can be built at compile
time. Combined with the \texttt{constexpr} \texttt{operator|}, this
allows an entire pipeline to be evaluated in a constant-expression
context. This is demonstrated later in the chapter.

A typical call site is
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}[](\allowbreak{}int x)\allowbreak{}\{ return x + 1; \allowbreak{}\})\allowbreak{}}.
The lambda's type is a unique, compiler-generated closure type, and
\texttt{F} is deduced to that closure type. Because each lambda has a
distinct type, two textually identical lambdas produce two distinct
\texttt{PipeStage} types; this rarely matters in practice, but it means
that a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}???\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
cannot be built without erasing the callable type first.

\section{The Free operator\textbar{}}\label{ch6-the-free-operator}

The entire runtime mechanism of the Pipeline feature is a single
function template. Quoted verbatim:

\begin{code}
template <typename T, typename F>
constexpr auto
operator| (T &&val, PipeStage<F> stage)
{
  return stage.fn (std::forward<T> (val));
}
\end{code}

The left operand is forwarded as \texttt{T\&\&}; the right operand is a
\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
taken by value. The body is a single call:
\texttt{stage.\allowbreak{}fn(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}forward\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}val)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
The stage's callable is invoked with the forwarded value, and the result
is returned. Because the return type is \texttt{auto}, it is deduced
from the callable's return type, which is how type-changing pipelines
are supported without any additional machinery.

The right operand is taken by value. This is consistent with the value
semantics of \texttt{PipeStage}: the stage is small (typically the size
of a lambda's capture) and is moved or copied into the operator. Because
the operator returns the result of the call rather than a new stage, the
stage is consumed by the application and does not survive the
expression. A stage stored in a named variable, however, is copied into
the operator and the original remains usable.

The left operand is forwarded perfectly. If the piped value is an
lvalue, the callable receives an lvalue reference; if the value is an
rvalue, the callable receives an rvalue reference. This means a stage
that moves from its argument (for example, a stage that converts a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} into a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} and
discards the original) can do so correctly when the pipeline is fed a
temporary.

Because the operator is a free function in the global namespace (with
\texttt{T} and \texttt{F} as unconstrained template parameters), it is
found by argument-dependent lookup whenever a
\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
appears as the right operand. The operator is also \texttt{constexpr},
which is what makes compile-time pipelines possible.

\section{The Pipeline Feature Tag}\label{ch6-the-pipeline-feature-tag}

The feature tag \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Pipeline} is the
hook through which a DSL acquires the \texttt{wrap} static member. Its
definition, quoted verbatim:

\begin{code}
struct Pipeline
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <typename T>
    static constexpr T
    wrap (T &&v)
    {
      return std::forward<T> (v);
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

The \texttt{Mixin} is injected into the DSL's CRTP base by the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
template described in Chapter 3. The \texttt{Derived} parameter is the
user's DSL type; \texttt{wrap} does not use it, but the parameter is
present for uniformity with other feature mixins described in Chapter 5.

The \texttt{wrap} member is a static, \texttt{constexpr}, identity
function. It takes a forwarding reference and returns
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}forward\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}v)\allowbreak{}}.
It does not construct a wrapper, tag the value, or alter its type. Its
sole purpose is to provide a uniform entry point that marks the
beginning of a pipeline. A call such as
\texttt{Proc:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap(\allowbreak{}10)\allowbreak{}}
reads as ``lift the value 10 into the pipeline,'' even though at the
type level nothing has changed.

Because \texttt{wrap} returns the value unchanged, the
\texttt{operator|} overload is the only thing that can follow it in a
chain. The expression
\texttt{Proc:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap(\allowbreak{}10)\allowbreak{} | dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}f)\allowbreak{}}
is therefore exactly \texttt{f(\allowbreak{}10)\allowbreak{}}. The
\texttt{wrap} call is, in a sense, documentation: it tells the reader
that what follows is a pipeline and not an ad-hoc use of
\texttt{operator|}. A DSL author who omits \texttt{wrap} and writes
\texttt{10 | dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}f)\allowbreak{}}
obtains the same result, but loses the visual cue and the uniformity
with other DSLs.

The \texttt{constexpr}-ness of \texttt{wrap} means a chain can begin in
a constant-expression context. Combined with the \texttt{constexpr}
\texttt{pipe(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} factory and the
\texttt{constexpr} \texttt{operator|}, an entire pipeline can be
evaluated at compile time, as shown later.

\section{Declaring a DSL With the Pipeline
Feature}\label{ch6-declaring-a-dsl-with-the-pipeline-feature}

To use the feature, a DSL lists
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Pipeline} among its features. The
minimal declaration is:

\begin{code}
struct Proc : dsl::DSL<Proc, dsl::Pipeline> {};
\end{code}

Here \texttt{Proc} is the user's DSL type, passed as the first template
argument (the \texttt{Derived} parameter of the CRTP base). The
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Pipeline} argument causes the
\texttt{Mixin:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap} static member to be
available on \texttt{Proc}. Once declared,
\texttt{Proc:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap} can be used to begin a
chain.

A DSL commonly combines several features. Chapter 1's \texttt{BasicDSL}
combines \texttt{Pipeline} with \texttt{Operators}:

\begin{code}
struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};
\end{code}

The order of feature tags in the parameter pack does not affect the
availability of \texttt{wrap}; the mixin injection is described in
Chapter 5. A DSL with both \texttt{Pipeline} and \texttt{Operators} can
build a pipeline and also compose predicates, as the example below
demonstrates.

\section{A First Worked Example}\label{ch6-a-first-worked-example}

The header's own example is a complete, compiling program. It mirrors
the canonical usage and shows the expected result.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"

struct Proc : dsl::DSL<Proc, dsl::Pipeline> {};

int main() {
  auto r = Proc::wrap(10)
           | dsl::pipe([](int x){ return x * 2; })
           | dsl::pipe([](int x){ return x + 1; });
  // r == 21
}
\end{code}

The chain begins with
\texttt{Proc:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap(\allowbreak{}10)\allowbreak{}},
which returns \texttt{10}. The first \texttt{|} applies the doubling
stage, yielding \texttt{20}. The second \texttt{|} applies the
incrementing stage, yielding \texttt{21}. The intermediate values are
prvalues of type \texttt{int}; no temporary objects beyond the
\texttt{PipeStage} wrappers are materialized.

The textual order of the stages matches the order of application. A
reader scanning the code from top to bottom sees the value enter the
pipeline, then each transformation in sequence. This is the principal
ergonomic benefit of the feature over nested calls.

\section{Type-Changing Pipelines}\label{ch6-type-changing-pipelines}

Because the \texttt{operator|} deduces its return type with
\texttt{auto}, each stage may return a different type. The pipeline's
type evolves as the value flows through the stages. Nothing in the
mechanism constrains the stages to a uniform type.

A chain that converts an integer to a floating-point value and then to a
string demonstrates this:

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

struct Proc : dsl::DSL<Proc, dsl::Pipeline> {};

int main() {
  auto s = Proc::wrap(7)
           | dsl::pipe([](int x){ return x * 2; })          // int -> int
           | dsl::pipe([](int x){ return x / 2.0; })        // int -> double
           | dsl::pipe([](double d) {                       // double -> std::string
               std::ostringstream os;
               os << d;
               return os.str();
             });
  // s == "7"
}
\end{code}

Each \texttt{|} produces a prvalue of a different type. The
\texttt{PipeStage} wrappers themselves have distinct types
(\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}lambda1\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}lambda2\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}lambda3\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}),
and the \texttt{operator|} instantiates separately for each pair of
operand types. The compiler generates a distinct call site for each
stage, with the appropriate argument and return types.

Type-changing chains are the rule rather than the exception in realistic
DSLs. A parsing pipeline might accept raw text, produce a token list,
then a syntax tree, then a value. A numeric pipeline might widen from
\texttt{int} to \texttt{double} to a fixed-point type. The Pipeline
feature imposes no constraint on these transitions; the only requirement
is that each stage's parameter type is compatible with the type produced
by the preceding stage.

\section{Value Semantics and
Forwarding}\label{ch6-value-semantics-and-forwarding}

A \texttt{PipeStage} holds its callable by value. The
\texttt{pipe(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} factory uses
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}decay\_\allowbreak{}t\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to strip references, so even when a stage is constructed from an lvalue
callable, the stage owns a copy. This means a stage is self-contained:
it can be moved, returned from a function, or stored in a container
without concern for the lifetime of the original callable.

The \texttt{operator|} takes the stage by value as well. When a stage is
passed to the operator, it is copied or moved into the operator's
parameter. If the stage was held in a named variable, the original is
unaffected (assuming the callable's copy constructor leaves the original
usable). If the stage was a temporary, it is moved. This makes pipelines
safe to write inline without considering aliasing.

The piped value, by contrast, is forwarded. The \texttt{T\&\&} parameter
in \texttt{operator|} is a forwarding reference, and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}forward\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}val)\allowbreak{}}
preserves the value category. A stage can therefore declare its
parameter as a value, an lvalue reference, or an rvalue reference, and
the call site will behave accordingly.

A stage that wishes to consume its argument can declare an
rvalue-reference parameter:

\begin{code}
auto drain = dsl::pipe([](std::string&& s) {
  // s is an rvalue; can be moved from
  return s.size();
});
auto n = Proc::wrap(std::string("hello")) | drain;
\end{code}

When the piped value is an rvalue (as above), the stage receives an
rvalue reference and may move from it. When the piped value is an
lvalue, the same stage would receive an lvalue reference; the stage's
parameter type must accept that. Mismatched value categories are
diagnosed at compile time by the language's usual reference-binding
rules.

\section{Reusable Stages}\label{ch6-reusable-stages}

Because stages have value semantics, a stage can be named and reused.
The same stage can be applied to multiple values without recompilation
or re-construction:

\begin{code}
auto square = dsl::pipe([](int x){ return x * x; });
auto a = Proc::wrap(3) | square;  // 9
auto b = Proc::wrap(4) | square;  // 16
\end{code}

Each use of \texttt{square} copies the \texttt{PipeStage} (and its
captured callable) into the \texttt{operator|}. The copy is cheap for a
captureless lambda, whose closure object is empty. For a lambda with
non-trivial capture, the cost is the cost of copying the capture; in
performance-sensitive code, the stage can be moved rather than copied by
naming it as an rvalue at the call site.

A stage can also be composed into a larger stage by capturing it in a
new lambda. This is not a special mechanism; it is ordinary lambda
capture applied to a \texttt{PipeStage} value. The captured stage is
copied into the new lambda's closure and invoked when the new lambda is
invoked.

\section{A Numeric Transform Chain}\label{ch6-a-numeric-transform-chain}

A realistic numeric pipeline applies a sequence of arithmetic and
rounding steps. The example below computes the square root of a sum of
squares after scaling.

\begin{code}
auto normalize = dsl::pipe([](int x){ return static_cast<double>(x); })
               | dsl::pipe([](double d){ return d / 255.0; });
\end{code}

Here \texttt{normalize} is not itself a \texttt{PipeStage}; it is the
result of \texttt{operator|}, which is the return value of the first
stage's callable applied to\ldots{} nothing. In fact this line does not
compile, because \texttt{operator|} between two \texttt{PipeStage}
objects is not defined. The Pipeline feature does not compose stages
with each other directly; stages are composed only by applying them to a
value.

To build a reusable multi-stage transform, the stages must be captured
into a single callable:

\begin{code}
auto normalize = [](int x) {
  return Proc::wrap(x)
         | dsl::pipe([](int v){ return static_cast<double>(v); })
         | dsl::pipe([](double d){ return d / 255.0; });
};
auto y = Proc::wrap(128) | dsl::pipe(normalize);  // 0.501961...
\end{code}

The \texttt{normalize} lambda wraps an inner pipeline and is itself
wrapped as a single \texttt{PipeStage}. This pattern is the idiomatic
way to name a multi-stage transform for reuse. The inner pipeline is
reconstructed each time the lambda is invoked, which is cheap for
captureless lambdas but worth noting for hot loops.

\section{String Processing Chains}\label{ch6-string-processing-chains}

String processing benefits from the left-to-right reading order, because
transformations are often described as a sequence of filtering and
shaping steps. The example below trims whitespace, lowercases, and
replaces spaces with underscores.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <algorithm>
#include <cctype>
#include <string>

struct Text : dsl::DSL<Text, dsl::Pipeline> {};

static std::string trim(const std::string& s) {
  auto b = s.begin(), e = s.end();
  while (b != e && std::isspace(static_cast<unsigned char>(*b))) ++b;
  while (e != b && std::isspace(static_cast<unsigned char>(*(e-1)))) --e;
  return std::string(b, e);
}

int main() {
  auto slug = Text::wrap(std::string("  Hello World  "))
    | dsl::pipe(trim)
    | dsl::pipe([](std::string s){
        std::transform(s.begin(), s.end(), s.begin(),
          [](unsigned char c){ return std::tolower(c); });
        return s;
      })
    | dsl::pipe([](std::string s){
        std::replace_if(s.begin(), s.end(),
          [](unsigned char c){ return std::isspace(c); }, '_');
        return s;
      });
  // slug == "hello_world"
}
\end{code}

Each stage is a self-contained function that accepts a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} and returns a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}. The pipeline reads top
to bottom as a recipe. Because each stage copies the string (or moves
it, where the return is a prvalue and the parameter is taken by value),
the chain is safe but not zero-allocation; a production version might
pass the string by reference to avoid copies. The Pipeline feature does
not prescribe the allocation strategy; that is the stage author's
concern.

\section{A CSV-Like Row Transform}\label{ch6-a-csv-like-row-transform}

A common DSL application is transforming tabular rows. The example below
treats a row as a vector of strings, parses the first field as an
integer, scales it, and re-emits the row with the scaled value.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Rows : dsl::DSL<Rows, dsl::Pipeline> {};

int main() {
  using Row = std::vector<std::string>;
  Row input{"3", "alice", "active"};

  auto out = Rows::wrap(input)
    | dsl::pipe([](Row r){
        int n = std::stoi(r[0]);
        r[0] = std::to_string(n * 10);
        return r;
      })
    | dsl::pipe([](Row r){
        r.push_back("processed");
        return r;
      });
  // out == {"30", "alice", "active", "processed"}
}
\end{code}

The first stage mutates the row in place and returns it. The second
stage appends a marker. Because each stage returns the row by value, the
chain does not alias the original \texttt{input} vector; \texttt{input}
is unchanged after the pipeline runs. (The first stage receives a copy
because \texttt{wrap} forwards the lvalue \texttt{input} as an lvalue
reference, and the stage takes its parameter by value, which copies.)

A more careful implementation might move the row through the chain to
avoid copies. This requires the stages to take their parameter by value
and the caller to feed an rvalue:

\begin{code}
auto out = Rows::wrap(std::move(input)) | dsl::pipe(/* ... */);
\end{code}

After \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}move}, \texttt{input} is in
a valid but unspecified state. The pipeline's stages receive the
moved-from vector and operate on it. This is the standard C++ approach
to moving a value through a chain of transformations, and the Pipeline
feature's perfect forwarding supports it without any special handling.

\section{An Inspection Stage}\label{ch6-an-inspection-stage}

Not every stage must transform its input. A logging or inspection stage
observes the value and returns it unchanged, allowing the chain to
continue. This is a direct consequence of the \texttt{operator|}
semantics: a stage that returns its argument unchanged is simply a stage
whose callable is an identity function with a side effect.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct Proc : dsl::DSL<Proc, dsl::Pipeline> {};

int main() {
  auto log = [](auto x) {
    std::cout << "stage: " << x << '\n';
    return x;
  };
  auto r = Proc::wrap(5)
    | dsl::pipe(log)
    | dsl::pipe([](int x){ return x * 2; })
    | dsl::pipe(log);
  // prints "stage: 5" then "stage: 10"; r == 10
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{log} stage is a generic lambda, so it accepts whatever type
flows into it. This makes it possible to insert the same logging stage
at multiple points in a type-changing pipeline. The stage's return type
is deduced as the argument's type, so it preserves the type of the
flowing value.

Inspection stages are valuable for debugging. They can be inserted
temporarily and removed without disturbing the rest of the pipeline.
Because the \texttt{operator|} is a free function, no special ``tap'' or
``peek'' combinator is needed; an identity-with-side-effect lambda is
sufficient.

\section{constexpr Pipelines}\label{ch6-constexpr-pipelines}

The \texttt{pipe(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} factory, the
\texttt{PipeStage} constructor, the \texttt{operator|}, and
\texttt{wrap} are all \texttt{constexpr}. A pipeline can therefore be
evaluated at compile time, producing a value that is then available as a
compile-time constant. This is useful for building lookup tables,
computing configuration constants, or asserting invariants at compile
time.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"

struct C : dsl::DSL<C, dsl::Pipeline> {};

constexpr int squared_plus_one(int x) {
  return C::wrap(x)
       | dsl::pipe([](int v){ return v * v; })
       | dsl::pipe([](int v){ return v + 1; });
}

static_assert(squared_plus_one(4) == 17);
\end{code}

The \texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert} verifies the result at compile
time. The lambdas are literal-friendly (their captures are empty, so
their closure types are literal types), and the \texttt{operator|} calls
are constant expressions. The entire chain is evaluated by the compiler,
and no runtime code is emitted for the pipeline itself.

A \texttt{constexpr} pipeline is subject to the usual constraints of
constant evaluation: no dynamic allocation, no I/O, no side effects that
the constant-evaluator cannot perform. The inspection-stage pattern
shown above cannot be used in a \texttt{constexpr} context because
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}cout} is not usable in constant
expressions. A stage that returns its input unchanged without side
effects, however, is fine.

Compile-time pipelines compose naturally with \texttt{consteval}
functions and with template metaprogramming. A \texttt{consteval}
function that builds a pipeline and returns its result forces the entire
evaluation to occur at compile time, which can be used to precompute
tables or to enforce that a configuration value is a compile-time
constant.

\section{Composition With Other Features:
Operators}\label{ch6-composition-with-other-features-operators}

The \texttt{BasicDSL} example from Chapter 1 combines \texttt{Pipeline}
with \texttt{Operators}. The pipeline produces a value, and the
predicates built with \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred} are then applied
to that value.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};

int main() {
  auto v = BasicDSL::wrap(5) | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; })
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x * 2; });
  auto gt5 = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x > 5; });
  auto even = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
  std::cout << v << " " << (gt5(v) && even(v)) << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The pipeline computes \texttt{v == 16}. The predicates, described in
Chapter 7, are then combined with ordinary logical operators. The two
features do not interfere: \texttt{Pipeline} provides \texttt{wrap} and
the \texttt{operator|} for \texttt{PipeStage}, while \texttt{Operators}
provides \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred} and the predicate-composition
operators described in Chapter 7. A user can also build a stage that
applies a predicate and returns a boolean, integrating the two features
within a single chain.

\section{Composition With Other Features:
CustomLiterals}\label{ch6-composition-with-other-features-customliterals}

The \texttt{MiniDSL} example from Chapter 17 combines \texttt{Pipeline}
with \texttt{CustomLiterals}. A literal is parsed, then piped through a
transformation.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct MiniDSL : dsl::DSL<MiniDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::CustomLiterals> {
  static constexpr auto literals = dsl::literal_set(
      dsl::lit<"_km">([](long double v) { return v * 1000.0L; }),
      dsl::lit<"_m">([](long double v) { return v; }));
};

int main() {
  auto meters = MiniDSL::parse_literal("2.5_km");
  auto shown = MiniDSL::wrap(meters) | dsl::pipe([](long double x) { return x + 10.0L; });
  std::cout << shown << "\n";
}
\end{code}

\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} returns
\texttt{2500.\allowbreak{}0L}. The pipeline then adds
\texttt{10.\allowbreak{}0L}, yielding \texttt{2510.\allowbreak{}0L}. The
\texttt{CustomLiterals} feature (Chapter 17) produces the value, and the
\texttt{Pipeline} feature transforms it. This pattern---parse, then pipe
through transformations---is a common shape for embedded DSLs that
interpret domain-specific notation.

The features compose without friction because each feature contributes
its own mixin members and its own free operators. \texttt{Pipeline}
contributes \texttt{wrap} and the \texttt{PipeStage} operator;
\texttt{CustomLiterals} contributes \texttt{literals} and
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal}. A DSL can list both and use either
at will.

\section{Operator Precedence and
Parenthesization}\label{ch6-operator-precedence-and-parenthesization}

The pipe operator \texttt{|} has lower precedence than arithmetic
operators but higher precedence than the logical operators \texttt{\&\&}
and \texttt{||}. In practice, this means that a lambda body that uses
\texttt{|} for bitwise OR must be parenthesized, and that a pipeline
expression combined with a logical predicate must be parenthesized. The
\texttt{BasicDSL} example parenthesizes
\texttt{gt5(\allowbreak{}v)\allowbreak{} \&\& even(\allowbreak{}v)\allowbreak{}}
because it is the argument to
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textless{}\allowbreak{}}, not because
of the pipeline, but the same principle applies.

A subtler precedence issue arises when a lambda is passed directly to
\texttt{pipe}. The expression
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}[](\allowbreak{}int x)\allowbreak{}\{ return x; \})\allowbreak{}}
parses correctly because the lambda is a primary expression. However, an
expression such as
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}[](\allowbreak{}int x)\allowbreak{}\{ return x; \} | \allowbreak{}other)\allowbreak{}}
would attempt to apply \texttt{operator|} between the lambda and
\texttt{other} before constructing the \texttt{PipeStage}. Because the
lambda is not a \texttt{PipeStage}, this is ill-formed. When in doubt,
parenthesize the lambda or name it first.

The bitwise-OR operator \texttt{|} is overloaded for \texttt{PipeStage}
on the right, but it is also the built-in bitwise OR for integer types.
A stage whose return type is an integer and which is followed by another
stage is unambiguous, because the right operand of \texttt{|} is a
\texttt{PipeStage}, not an integer. But a stage that returns an integer
followed by an integer (without a \texttt{PipeStage}) is interpreted as
bitwise OR. This is rarely a problem in practice, because a pipeline is
a sequence of
\texttt{| pipe(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
applications, but it is worth knowing.

\section{Reference Captures and
Lifetime}\label{ch6-reference-captures-and-lifetime}

A lambda stage may capture variables by reference. When it does, the
stage holds references to the captured variables, and the pipeline's
correctness depends on those variables outliving the stage's use.
Because stages are typically constructed inline and consumed within the
same expression, this is usually safe. But a stage stored in a variable
and used later, or a stage used in a pipeline that outlives the
enclosing scope, can dangle.

\begin{code}
auto make_stage() {
  int factor = 10;
  return dsl::pipe([&factor](int x){ return x * factor; });
  // factor is destroyed when make_stage returns; the stage dangles
}
\end{code}

The stage returned from \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}stage} holds a
reference to a destroyed local. Using it is undefined behavior. The fix
is to capture by value:
\texttt{[factor](\allowbreak{}int x)\allowbreak{}\{ return x * fac\allowbreak{}tor; \}}.
The value semantics of \texttt{PipeStage} then ensure the factor is
owned by the stage.

For non-copyable captures, or for captures that must be shared between
stages, a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}shared\_\allowbreak{}ptr} can be
captured by value. The stage then holds a copy of the shared pointer,
and the referenced object lives as long as any stage holds it. This is
the same discipline that applies to any lambda with reference captures;
the Pipeline feature introduces no additional lifetime hazards, but it
also provides no additional lifetime safety.

\section{Lifetime of Temporaries in the
Chain}\label{ch6-lifetime-of-temporaries-in-the-chain}

Each intermediate value in a pipeline is a prvalue. The language's
temporary materialization rules ensure that each prvalue lives until the
end of the full expression that contains it. Because the
\texttt{operator|} consumes its left operand within the expression, the
intermediate values are alive for exactly as long as they are needed.

A stage that returns a reference to its argument (for example, a stage
that mutates in place and returns a reference) can extend a temporary's
lifetime across one \texttt{|}, but the lifetime does not extend beyond
the full expression. This is the standard C++ rule; the Pipeline feature
does not alter it. In particular, binding the result of a pipeline to a
reference extends the lifetime of the final prvalue, but not of any
intermediate prvalue.

For pipelines that produce containers, the move semantics of the
containers ensure that the final result is constructed efficiently. A
chain
\texttt{wrap(\allowbreak{}s)\allowbreak{} | pipe(\allowbreak{}f1)\allowbreak{} | pipe(\allowbreak{}f2)\allowbreak{}}
where each stage returns a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} by value will move the
string through the chain if the stages take their parameters by value
and return prvalues. No copies are mandated by the Pipeline feature; the
copy/move behavior is determined entirely by the stages' signatures and
the language's rules.

\section{A Larger Example: Multi-Stage
Validation}\label{ch6-a-larger-example-multi-stage-validation}

A validation pipeline applies a series of checks to a value, aborting on
failure. Because the Pipeline feature does not provide short-circuiting,
a validation pipeline typically encodes failure as a value (a
\texttt{Result}, an \texttt{optional}, or a sentinel) and lets each
stage pass failures through unchanged. Chapter 22 describes the
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
type, which is well suited to this pattern.

A sketch using \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}:

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <optional>

struct Valid : dsl::DSL<Valid, dsl::Pipeline> {};

int main() {
  auto check_positive = [](int x) -> std::optional<int> {
    return x > 0 ? std::optional<int>{x} : std::nullopt;
  };
  auto check_even = [](std::optional<int> o) -> std::optional<int> {
    if (!o) return std::nullopt;
    return *o % 2 == 0 ? o : std::nullopt;
  };
  auto r = Valid::wrap(4)
    | dsl::pipe(check_positive)
    | dsl::pipe(check_even);
  // r has value 4
}
\end{code}

Each stage accepts an
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and returns one. A \texttt{nullopt} flows through the remaining stages
unchanged. The pipeline does not short-circuit---it executes every
stage---but the result is the same as if it did, because the
failure-propagating stages are cheap. Chapter 21 describes monadic
helpers for \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} that can
make this pattern more concise.

This pattern illustrates a general principle: the Pipeline feature
provides control flow (sequential application) but not branching.
Branching is encoded in the data, not in the pipeline. A stage that
needs to choose between two transformations must do so internally and
return the appropriate value.

\section{Composing Stages Into Named
Transforms}\label{ch6-composing-stages-into-named-transforms}

A long pipeline can be broken into named transforms by wrapping
sub-chains in lambdas, as shown earlier. This improves readability and
allows reuse. The pattern is:

\begin{code}
auto parse = [](std::string s) {
  return Proc::wrap(s)
    | dsl::pipe([](std::string t){ /* ... */ return t; })
    | dsl::pipe([](std::string t){ /* ... */ return t; });
};
auto emit = [](std::string s) {
  return Proc::wrap(s)
    | dsl::pipe([](std::string t){ /* ... */ return t; });
};
auto result = Proc::wrap(input)
  | dsl::pipe(parse)
  | dsl::pipe(emit);
\end{code}

Each named transform is a single-argument callable, so it can be wrapped
in a \texttt{PipeStage} and used as one stage of a larger chain. The
inner pipelines are reconstructed on each invocation, but for non-hot
paths this is negligible. The named transforms can be tested in
isolation and reused across pipelines.

This decomposition mirrors the structure of a Unix shell pipeline, where
\texttt{parse | transfor\allowbreak{}m | emit} is expressed as a
sequence of named filters. The Pipeline feature brings the same
ergonomics to C++ value transformations, with the type system checking
the compatibility of each stage's input and output types.

\section{Pitfalls Summary}\label{ch6-pitfalls-summary}

The Pipeline feature is small, but a few pitfalls recur. The first is
precedence: lambdas combined with \texttt{|} must be parenthesized when
the lambda is not the sole argument to \texttt{pipe}. The second is
reference capture: a stage that captures by reference dangles if it
outlives the captured variable. The third is lifetime of intermediates:
the feature follows C++ rules exactly, so a stage that returns a
dangling reference is undefined behavior, just as it would be outside a
pipeline.

A fourth pitfall is the assumption that the pipeline short-circuits. It
does not. Every stage is executed in sequence, regardless of the values
flowing through. A stage that should not execute on a failure value must
be written to detect the failure and pass it through. A fifth pitfall is
the assumption that \texttt{|} between two \texttt{PipeStage} objects
composes them; it does not, because no such overload exists. Stages are
composed by applying them to a value, or by capturing one stage inside
another's lambda.

Finally, a pitfall specific to \texttt{wrap}: because \texttt{wrap} is
an identity function, it is easy to forget that it does nothing. A user
who writes
\texttt{Proc:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap(\allowbreak{}x)\allowbreak{}}
expecting some transformation to occur will be surprised that \texttt{x}
is returned unchanged. The purpose of \texttt{wrap} is to mark the start
of a pipeline and to provide a uniform entry point; the transformation
is done by the stages that follow.

\section{Summary}\label{ch6-summary}

The Pipeline feature provides left-to-right function composition through
a single overloaded \texttt{operator|}. The
\texttt{PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
wrapper holds a callable by value; the
\texttt{pipe(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} factory constructs a wrapper
with deduced type; the free
\texttt{operator|(\allowbreak{}T\&\&,\allowbreak{} PipeStage\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
applies the callable to its left operand and returns the result. The
\texttt{Pipeline} feature tag contributes
\texttt{Mixin:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap}, a \texttt{constexpr}
identity function that marks the start of a chain and returns its
argument unchanged.

The mechanism is entirely value-based: stages are moved or copied into
the operator, and the piped value is perfectly forwarded. Each stage may
return a different type, so type-changing pipelines are first-class.
Because the operators are \texttt{constexpr}, pipelines can be evaluated
at compile time. The feature composes naturally with \texttt{Operators}
(Chapter 7), \texttt{CustomLiterals} (Chapter 17), and the monadic types
of Chapters 20 through 22. The principal pitfalls are operator
precedence, reference-capture lifetimes, and the absence of
short-circuiting, all of which are managed by ordinary C++ discipline.
